Z||000006||batboy||11-24-2000||10:33 PM||||MM, you don't currently have a sound card? If you do, chances are it does have an input, even if it's only a mic input, which will still work. You just need a converter cable that has a pair of female shielded RCA plugs that Y to a single minature stereo plug. You'll also need software to record the analog signal into .wav files onto your harddrive. You'll also get a fair amount of tape hiss recorded too. There is available software to help remove some of this hiss. I've recorded some of my record albums and let me tell you, .wav files are big and take lots of space. Once you burn them to CD, you'll probably want to delete the files off your harddrive before recording the next tape.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||11-24-2000||10:41 PM||||MM, this place sells the OEM Sound Blaster Live cards for $42 not including shipping. I've bought several things from them in the past. They'll probably try to sell you a $5 audio cable with the card too, but your CD-ROM/CD-RW usually come with those cables, so you might not need one. If you can play an audio CD and hear music, you already have the audio cable.<P> <A HREF="http://www.compuplus.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.compuplus.com/</A> <p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 11-24-2000).]||||reg||1
